# The `calc` server as one rootless image. Less boring than the other three, for one reason: this
# server's answers are *computed* rather than looked up, so what it carries is a numerical stack —
# tblite's compiled GFN Hamiltonians, RDKit, and scipy's LAPACK — instead of a data directory.
#
# **On the `xtb` and `crest` binaries — this image now ships both**, which is the change
# `D-2026-08-26-a-sampler-nobody-ships-is-a-refusal-with-a-manual` made. They are compiled Fortran
# programs distributed through conda-forge rather than PyPI, so a Python dependency cannot express
# them and the stage below installs them the way they are actually distributed. What their absence
# cost was not evenly spread: `xtb` is an optimisation, since `tblite` carries the same GFN1/GFN2
# Hamiltonians in-process (it buys ANCopt, measured ~7-9x on 76-118 atoms, and GFN-FF), while
# **`crest` has no in-process substitute at all** — without it `search_conformer_ensemble`,
# `search_binding_modes` and every Chemclaw3 composite over them refused, and the four searches
# were shipped untested because no machine that ran the suite could run one.
#
# The versions are pinned rather than floating: `crest --version` and `xtb --version` are both
# interpolated into `calc_version`, so an unpinned build would re-key every cached calculation on a
# base-image rebuild.
#
# **Licence.** `crest` is GPL-3.0 and `xtb` is LGPL-3.0. Both are invoked as separate processes over
# files and neither is linked into this codebase, so the analysis is unchanged from
# `engine/crest_cli.py`'s — but *shipping* them in an image is distribution, and the obligations
# (offer of source, licence texts) attach to whoever publishes it. That is a product decision, taken
# deliberately here rather than inherited: see the ADR and `README.md`.
FROM python:3.11-slim AS sampling

ARG CREST_VERSION=3.0.2
ARG XTB_VERSION=6.7.1
ENV MAMBA_ROOT_PREFIX=/opt/mamba

# `curl` and `bzip2` are the whole toolchain: micromamba is one static binary, and it installs the
# two programs plus their Fortran and OpenMP runtimes into a self-contained prefix whose libraries
# are found through RPATH — so the final stage copies one directory and needs no LD_LIBRARY_PATH.
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl bzip2 ca-certificates \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && curl -sSL https://micro.mamba.pm/api/micromamba/linux-64/latest \
       | tar -xj -C /usr/local bin/micromamba \
    && micromamba create -y -p /opt/sampling -c conda-forge \
       "crest=${CREST_VERSION}" "xtb=${XTB_VERSION}" \
    && micromamba clean --all --yes \
    && rm -rf /opt/mamba

FROM python:3.11-slim AS build

WORKDIR /build
# **The lock, because an image that re-resolves is an image nobody audited.**
# `make deps-audit` reads `uv.lock`; until this line existed no Containerfile in this repository
# copied it, so all seven re-resolved independently from the open lower bounds in each
# `pyproject.toml`. Measured 2026-08-28 against the `rxnpredict` image: 11 of 100 packages differed
# from the export the audit reads, `pandas` by a major version — so the supply-chain gate proved a
# property of a file and not of anything shipped
# (`D-2026-09-13-an-audit-of-a-lockfile-no-image-reads-audits-nothing`).
COPY pyproject.toml uv.lock /build/
COPY packages/mcp_server_kit /build/packages/mcp_server_kit
COPY servers/calc /build/servers/calc

# Wheels, not an editable install: what ships is what a `pip install` of the two packages produces,
# which is also what the tests import. RDKit, tblite, scipy and numpy all ship manylinux wheels, so
# none of those four is compiled here and no Fortran toolchain is needed in this stage.
#
# **`geometric` is the one that is compiled here — it is pure Python, but it is *built* — and that
# is why the build backend is installed here at all.** It publishes no wheel on PyPI, for any
# release, and its sdist has no `pyproject.toml` at all: a legacy `setup.py` beside a bundled
# `versioneer.py`. Under pip's default build isolation that means pip resolves *its own* build
# requirements (`setuptools`, `wheel`) from PyPI at the moment of the build — versions chosen that
# day, no hashes, nothing in `uv.lock` — and then runs `setup.py` under them. Measured: the
# `--require-hashes` form still prints `Installing build dependencies`, and the same sdist built
# under the backend pip fetched and under the locked one produced wheels of **different sizes** —
# 408,349 bytes against 408,351 — so what this image ships depends on what pip happened to install
# that morning. So the build backend is installed **first**, from `uv.lock`'s `build` dependency
# group with `--require-hashes`, and the wheel pass runs `--no-build-isolation` so pip uses that one
# and fetches nothing
# (`D-2026-09-16-a-dependency-with-no-wheel-builds-under-whatever-pip-fetches-that-day`).
# `--no-build-isolation` is safe to apply to the whole requirements file because everything else in
# this closure ships a wheel and is therefore never built. *Which* entries are built is derived from
# `uv.lock` rather than written down here, by
# `tests/test_fleet.py::test_a_sdist_only_dependency_builds_under_a_pinned_backend` — which requires
# this shape of any server whose closure grows a wheel-less dependency, and refuses it of any server
# whose closure has none.
#
# Two `pip wheel` passes with a backend install in front of the first, and the split is the point.
# The first wheel pass resolves *nothing*: every third-party version and hash comes from `uv.lock`
# by way of `uv export --frozen`, so what this image installs is what `make deps-audit` read.
# The second builds the two workspace
# packages `--no-deps`, because their dependencies are already in the wheelhouse and letting
# pip look again is exactly the re-resolution this replaced. It is `--no-build-isolation` too, for
# the same reason the first pass is: `hatchling` is in the `build` group and therefore in the lock,
# so both passes execute a backend the audit read
# (`D-2026-09-18-a-backend-that-writes-the-metadata-is-a-dependency-of-the-wheel`).
#
# `uv` is pinned only by a range: `--frozen` means the *resolution* cannot drift whichever
# version runs, and a `uv` that changed this CLI would fail the build loudly rather than
# quietly ship a different closure.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip "uv>=0.8.17,<1" \
    && uv export --frozen --package chemclaw-mcp-calc --no-dev --no-emit-workspace \
         --format requirements-txt -o /build/requirements.txt \
    && uv export --frozen --only-group build --no-emit-workspace \
         --format requirements-txt -o /build/build-requirements.txt \
    && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --require-hashes -r /build/build-requirements.txt \
    && python -m pip wheel --no-cache-dir --no-build-isolation --wheel-dir /wheels \
         --require-hashes -r /build/requirements.txt \
    && python -m pip wheel --no-cache-dir --no-deps --no-build-isolation --wheel-dir /wheels ./packages/mcp_server_kit ./servers/calc

FROM python:3.11-slim

# A fixed high UID rather than a name: OpenShift assigns an arbitrary UID from the namespace range,
# and the process must own nothing it needs to write.
ARG UID=1001
RUN useradd --uid ${UID} --create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin app

COPY --from=build /wheels /wheels
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --no-index --find-links=/wheels \
        mcp-server-kit chemclaw-mcp-calc \
    && rm -rf /wheels

# The samplers, read-only to the running user: nothing in this image writes into them, and a CREST
# run works in a fresh temporary directory (`crest_cli`), never in its own installation.
COPY --from=sampling /opt/sampling /opt/sampling
ENV PATH=/opt/sampling/bin:$PATH

# **The `xtb` binary is installed and is deliberately not the default backend.** `CHEMCLAW_XTB_ENGINE`
# defaults to `auto`, which resolves to the binary the moment one is on PATH — and the backend is
# interpolated into `calc_version`, so this image would otherwise change, on the day it is deployed:
#
#   opt-GFN2-xTB+tblite-0.7.0/...   ->   opt-GFN2-xTB+xtb-6.7.1/tblite-0.7.0/...
#
# Measured, both strings, with and without the binary on PATH. Three things follow from that one
# character difference, none of them announced: every row already in `calculation_results` misses
# forever, every reconciled residual in Chemclaw3's calibration ledger becomes unreachable
# (`predictions` is keyed on `calc_version` and read with an exact predicate, so
# `calculator_trust("pka")` would report a confident `UNCALIBRATED`, n=0), and `predict_pka`'s base
# branch — which relaxes both species through this backend — would compute numbers its calibration
# was not fitted on. The slope and intercept `pka.py` ships were fitted through the *tblite* path.
#
# So the binary ships available rather than active: ANCopt and GFN-FF are one environment variable
# away for a deployment that wants them and has decided to recompute, and CREST is unaffected either
# way because it carries its own GFN implementation. Adding a backend to an image must not silently
# re-key a cache and a calibration.
ENV CHEMCLAW_XTB_ENGINE=tblite

USER ${UID}
EXPOSE 8860

# The egress guard is armed on import and is left at its default. `MCP_EGRESS_ALLOW` is not set here,
# and setting it in a deployment is a decision to be argued for — the NetworkPolicy beside this file
# denies egress at the network layer regardless.
ENV MCP_EGRESS_GUARD=on

# The whole image is one process on one event loop, and the numerical stack underneath is threaded:
# LAPACK and tblite's OpenMP will each size themselves to the *node*, not to the container's CPU
# limit, and then fight each other. One thread per library, with concurrency coming from the request
# level (`asyncio.to_thread`) where the server can actually see it. Raise deliberately, on a pod
# sized for it.
ENV OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 \
    OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 \
    MKL_NUM_THREADS=1

# **A CREST run is the one thing in this image that should use more than one core, and it has to be
# told so here.** The three variables above bind the in-process stack, and they do not reach the
# sampler: `crest_cli` runs it with a scrubbed environment of four allow-listed variables, so
# `OMP_NUM_THREADS` is not inherited. Left unset, CREST's own OpenMP sizes itself from
# `/proc/cpuinfo` — the *node's* core count, which a container CPU limit does not change — and one
# search would then saturate a host it was never scheduled for. This is the bound; a pod sized for
# heavier sampling raises it deliberately.
ENV CHEMCLAW_CREST_THREADS=4

# The build this image is. `mcp_server_kit.app.server_revision` reads `MCP_SERVER_REVISION` and
# carries it into the `initialize()` handshake's `serverInfo.version` and into `/healthz`, so a
# caller learns which build answered without a second endpoint. Left unsupplied it reports
# `"unknown"` — which is exactly what Chemclaw3's own revision field did for eight months, the
# field and its test both present and no build ever setting the variable. So `tests/test_fleet.py`
# asserts *this pair, in this file*: a provenance record nothing fills is one that quietly says
# nothing. The release pipeline passes `--build-arg CHEMCLAW_REVISION=$(git rev-parse HEAD)`.
ARG CHEMCLAW_REVISION=unknown
ENV MCP_SERVER_REVISION=${CHEMCLAW_REVISION}

# The startup probe Chemclaw3's connector health check uses is GET /healthz on this port.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --start-period=10s \
    CMD python -c "import urllib.request,sys; sys.exit(0 if urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8860/healthz').status==200 else 1)"

CMD ["uvicorn", "chemclaw_mcp_calc.app:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8860"]
